tel 1.0

base mandible
import soundness

info   JVM class files as values: methods, bytecode with simulated stacks, declaration surfaces and supertype closures
detail
    _Mandible_ reads JVM class files — the `.class` output of `scalac` or `javac` — into
    immutable, typed values, so that a program can inspect what the compiler and the optimizer
    actually produced rather than assume it. It reads the files through the JDK's own
    `java.lang.classfile` API and presents three views of a class, each a value:

    - `Classfile` is the **bytecode** view: the methods of a class, each with its `Bytecode`,
      a list of `Bytecode.Instruction`s decoded to a typed `Bytecode.Opcode`, carrying the
      source line it came from and a simulation of the operand stack after it runs. It
      renders as a coloured table at a terminal, and `disassemble` compiles a quoted block
      and reads its bytecode straight back.
    - `ClassSurface` is the **declaration** view: the names, descriptors, access flags,
      supertypes and generic signatures a compiled consumer links against, and nothing that
      can legitimately differ between two builds of the same source.
    - `ClasspathIndex` resolves JVM internal class names to their `ClassSurface`s over a set
      of classes held in memory plus a classpath, and walks a class's supertype closure.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

    val classfile: Optional[Classfile] = Classfile[StackTrace]
    val bytecode: Optional[Bytecode] =
      classfile.let(_.methods.seek(_.name == t"rewrite")).let(_.bytecode)

    bytecode.let(_.instructions.map(_.opcode.show))
    ```

    A class file is found through the `Classloader` given in scope, chosen by name from the
    `classloaders` package (`import soundness.*` imports no givens), or read from any stream
    of bytes with `read[Classfile]`. The library has a single source set, `mandible.core`,
    which is JVM-only: it depends on `java.lang.classfile`, on the staging compiler for
    `disassemble` and on the JVM classpath. Everything is exported to the `soundness`
    umbrella.

    Rendering `Bytecode` to a terminal needs a `BytecodePalette` in scope, a `Palette` naming
    the colours for source references, source lines and the stack outline; no default
    palette is provided, so a program defines its own.

refer mandible.Classfile
refer mandible.Bytecode
refer mandible.ClassSurface
refer mandible.disassemble

entry .Classfile   a parsed JVM class file: its methods, their bytecode and its source attributes
  detail
      A `Classfile` wraps the bytes of one `.class` file, parsed on first use through the
      JDK's `java.lang.classfile` API. It is found on the classpath by name or by type, through
      the `Classloader` given in scope, or read from any source of bytes — a file, a resource,
      a stream — with `read[Classfile]`, since it is `Aggregable by Data`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      Classfile[StackTrace].let(_.methods.map(_.name))
      Classfile(t"scala/Option.class").let(_.sourceFile)
      ```

      Its members are the parts a disassembler and a stack-trace resolver need: `#methods`,
      each a `Classfile.Method` whose `bytecode` is decoded on request; `#sourceFile`, the
      `SourceFile` attribute; and `#sourceDebugExtension`, the JSR-45 SMAP which maps the
      synthetic line numbers of inlined code back to where it was written. The declaration
      surface of a class — fields, flags, supertypes, signatures — is the separate
      `ClassSurface` view.

      A `Classfile` holds no capability: its bytes are copied in at construction, so the
      value may be kept, shared and passed freely.
  refer mandible.Bytecode
  refer mandible.ClassSurface
  refer mandible.disassemble
  entry .Error   raised when a class file cannot be read
      Error 293: _there was an error reading the classfile_. Declared for readers of class
      files to raise; the `Classfile` constructor itself defers parsing until a member is
      first used, and `Classfile.apply` gives `Unset` for a class the classloader does not
      have rather than raising.
  entry .aggregable   reads a `Classfile` from any stream of bytes, as `read[Classfile]`
      The `Aggregable by Data` given which lets a file, a classpath resource or any other
      `Readable` source be read as a class file: the whole stream is collected into one `Data`
      and wrapped. The bytes are not parsed until a member of the result is used.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val classfile: Classfile = cp"/scala/Option.class".read[Classfile]
      ```
  entry .apply   the class file of a resource name, or of a type, through the `Classloader` in scope
      `Classfile(name)` reads the classpath resource at `name` — a JVM internal name with its
      extension, `scala/Option.class` — through the `Classloader` given in scope, and
      `Classfile[T]` finds the class of `T` by its `ClassTag` and reads the resource its
      binary name denotes, so a nested class resolves to `Outer$Nested.class`. Both give
      `Unset` when the classloader has no such resource.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

      Classfile[StackTrace].let(_.methods.size)
      Classfile(t"scala/Option.class").let(_.methods.size)
      ```

      The type form takes a `ClassTag`, so it works for a class or an object's type, but not
      for an unapplied type constructor; `Classloader[T]` in hellenism is the companion
      operation for finding the classloader itself.
  entry #sourceFile   the `SourceFile` attribute: the name of the source the class was compiled from
      The file name alone, `Option.scala`, with no directory, as the compiler records it; or
      `Unset` when the class file carries no such attribute, as some generated classes do not.
      It is the file name a stack trace reports, and `Bytecode#sourceFile` carries it on to
      the rendered listing.
  entry #sourceDebugExtension   the JSR-45 `SourceDebugExtension` attribute, as text, if present
      The body of the attribute is an SMAP, a source map which translates the synthetic line
      numbers the compiler allocates to inlined code back to the file and line they were
      written at, level by level. It is `Unset` for a class with no inlining. A stack-trace
      resolver reads it to give an inlined frame its real call-site line; `Smap.parse` in
      digression decodes the text.
  entry #methods   the methods of the class, in class-file order, each a `Classfile.Method`
      Constructors appear as `<init>`, a static initializer as `<clinit>`, and the compiler's
      synthetic methods — lambda bodies, bridges, default getters — under their compiled
      names. The list is computed once, on first use, and parsing happens then.
  entry #Method   one method of a class file, with its name and its decoded bytecode
    detail
        A `Classfile.Method` is created by `Classfile#methods` and is not constructed directly.
        Its `#name` is the method's compiled name, and `#bytecode` decodes its `Code`
        attribute into a `Bytecode` value: every instruction with its offset, the source line
        it belongs to, and the operand stack as simulated from the method's stack-map frames.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

        Classfile[StackTrace].let(_.methods.seek(_.name == t"rewrite")).let(_.bytecode)
        ```
    entry #name   the method's name as the class file spells it
        The compiled name, not the source name: a constructor is `<init>`, an operator such as
        `+` is `$plus`, and a lambda body is `$anonfun$…`. `StackTrace.rewrite` in digression
        turns such a name back toward its source spelling.
    entry #bytecode   the method's `Code` attribute decoded to a `Bytecode`, or `Unset` for an abstract method
        Decodes every instruction of the method to a `Bytecode.Opcode`, resolving branch
        targets to byte offsets, and threads a simulation of the operand stack through them:
        the stack after each instruction is computed by `Bytecode.Opcode#transform`, and reset
        at every label from the method's `StackMapTable` frames, so the stack at a branch
        target is what the verifier records, not what the fall-through path left. Line
        numbers from the `LineNumberTable` are attached to the first instruction of each line.
        A method with no code — abstract or native — gives `Unset`.

        The result carries the class's `sourceFile`, and the method's declared `maxStack` and
        `maxLocals`.

entry .Bytecode   a method's instructions, each decoded, placed on its source line, with its simulated operand stack
  detail
      A `Bytecode` is the decoded `Code` attribute of one method: its `#instructions`, each a
      `Bytecode.Instruction` pairing a typed `Bytecode.Opcode` with its byte `offset`, the
      source `line` it begins, and the operand `stack` — a list of `Bytecode.Frame`s — as it
      stands after the instruction runs; plus the method's declared `#maxStack` and
      `#maxLocals` and the class's `#sourceFile`. It is produced by `Classfile.Method#bytecode`
      from a class on the classpath, or by `disassemble` from a quoted block compiled on the
      spot, and may also be built by hand from `Instruction`s for analysis.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A class file, found through the classloader in scope
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

            val classfile: Optional[Classfile] = Classfile[StackTrace]
      frame
        label One of its methods, by name
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

            val classfile: Optional[Classfile] = Classfile[StackTrace]
            val method: Optional[Classfile.Method] =
              classfile.let(_.methods.seek(_.name == t"rewrite"))
      frame
        label Its bytecode, listed as opcodes
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

            val classfile: Optional[Classfile] = Classfile[StackTrace]
            val method: Optional[Classfile.Method] =
              classfile.let(_.methods.seek(_.name == t"rewrite"))
            val bytecode: Optional[Bytecode] = method.let(_.bytecode)

            bytecode.let(_.instructions.map(_.opcode.show))   // List(t"a·load₀", t"invoke·virtual …", …)
      frame
        label One instruction, decoded: its offset, its line and the stack it leaves
        code
            import soundness.*
            import classloaders.threadContextClassloader

            val classfile: Optional[Classfile] = Classfile[StackTrace]
            val method: Optional[Classfile.Method] =
              classfile.let(_.methods.seek(_.name == t"rewrite"))
            val bytecode: Optional[Bytecode] = method.let(_.bytecode)

            bytecode.let(_.instructions.seek(_.opcode == Bytecode.Opcode.Aload0)).let: instruction =>
              (instruction.offset, instruction.line, instruction.stack.let(_.map(_.show)))
      ##
      ```

      The operand stack is a simulation, not a measurement: `Bytecode.Opcode#transform` pushes
      and pops what each opcode is defined to, and the method's `StackMapTable` resets it at
      every branch target. A reference on the stack is a `Frame.L(className)` where the
      instruction that pushed it names a class, and `Frame.L(t"?")` where it does not.

      Rendering needs a `BytecodePalette` in scope: `bytecode.teletype` is a four-column table
      of source position, offset, opcode (coloured by `Opcode#cost`) and stack, and
      `bytecode.printable` prints it. `#linearize` follows the static and effectively-static
      calls a method makes into their callees' bytecode, to a bounded depth, which is how a
      hot path's full instruction sequence is seen in one listing.
  refer mandible.Classfile
  refer mandible.disassemble
  refer mandible.BytecodePalette
  entry .teletypeable   renders bytecode as a coloured table: source line, offset, opcode and stack
      The `Teletypeable` given, needing a `BytecodePalette`. One row per instruction: the
      source file and line (in the palette's `bytecode` and `sourceCode` colours) where the
      instruction begins a line, the byte offset as subscripts, the opcode coloured by its
      `Opcode#cost`, and the simulated stack, outlined in the palette's `outline` colour. The
      table is laid out for 160 columns.
  entry .printable   prints a bytecode listing, as `Printable`
      Renders `.teletypeable`'s table for the terminal it is printed to; needs a
      `BytecodePalette`.
  entry .stack   renders a simulated operand stack, a `List[Bytecode.Frame]`, in one line
      The `Teletypeable` given for a stack, needing a `BytecodePalette`: frames are shown
      bottom-first, separated by `┊` and outlined with `┃` in the palette's `outline` colour.
  entry .Frame   one slot of the operand stack or one verification type: a primitive, a reference or an array
    detail
        The cases are the JVM's descriptor letters for the primitives — `Z` (boolean), `B`
        (byte), `C` (char), `S` (short), `I` (int), `J` (long), `F` (float), `D` (double) —
        with `L(name)` for a reference to a class, by its binary name (`java.lang.String`),
        and `Array(frame)` for an array of another frame. A reference whose class is unknown is
        `L(t"?")`; the verifier's special types appear as `L(t"null")`, `L(t"this")`,
        `L(t"top")` and `L(t"uninit")`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Bytecode.Frame.fromFieldDescriptor(t"[Ljava/lang/String;")
        // Bytecode.Frame.Array(Bytecode.Frame.L(t"java.lang.String"))
        ```

        A `Frame` shows as its letter, as `*name` for a reference and `[frame]` for an array.
    entry .showable   renders a frame: a primitive's letter, `*name` for a reference, `[frame]` for an array
    entry .parseOne   reads one type from a descriptor at a position, giving the frame and the position after it
        `Frame.parseOne(descriptor, cursor)` decodes the type whose descriptor begins at
        `cursor` — a letter, a `[`-prefixed array or an `L…;` class — and returns it with the
        index just past it, for a caller walking a method descriptor's parameter list. An
        unexpected character is a panic, since a descriptor from a class file is well-formed.
    entry .fromFieldDescriptor   the frame of a field descriptor, `I` or `Ljava/lang/String;`
        Decodes a whole field descriptor to one `Frame`, with `/` in a class name rewritten to
        `.`.
    entry .fromVerificationType   the frame of a `StackMapTable` verification type
        Maps the JDK's `VerificationTypeInfo` to a `Frame`: the simple types to their
        primitives, an object type to `L(name)` (or an array frame when the class is an array
        class), `null`, `uninitializedThis` and `top` to `L(t"null")`, `L(t"this")` and
        `L(t"top")`, and an uninitialized object to `L(t"uninit")`.
  entry .Descriptor   a method descriptor parsed into its parameter frames and its result
    detail
        `Bytecode.Descriptor.parse(t"(Ljava/lang/String;I)V")` gives a `Descriptor` whose
        `#args` are the frames `L(java.lang.String)` and `I` and whose `#result` is `Unset`,
        since `V` is void. The instruction decoder uses it to know how many operands an
        invocation pops and whether it pushes a result.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val descriptor = Bytecode.Descriptor.parse(t"([[Ljava/lang/Object;J)Z")
        descriptor.args.size     // 2
        descriptor.result        // Bytecode.Frame.Z
        ```
    entry .parse   reads a `(…)R` method descriptor into a `Descriptor`
        The text must begin with `(`; the parameter types up to `)` become `#args`, and the
        return type `#result`, with `V` as `Unset`. A malformed descriptor panics.
    entry #args   the parameter types, in order
    entry #result   the return type, or `Unset` for `void`
  entry .Instruction   one decoded instruction: its opcode, source line, resulting stack and offset
    detail
        The element of `Bytecode#instructions`. `#line` is set on the first instruction of
        each source line and `Unset` on the rest; `#stack` is the simulated operand stack
        after the instruction, or `Unset` once the simulation has been lost; `#offset` is the
        instruction's position in the method's code, in bytes, which is what branch targets
        and `Bytecode#effectivelyStaticCalls` refer to.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val instruction =
          Bytecode.Instruction(Bytecode.Opcode.Iconst1, Unset, List(Bytecode.Frame.I), 0)
        ```
    entry #opcode   the instruction, as a `Bytecode.Opcode`
    entry #line   the source line this instruction begins, or `Unset` if it continues one
    entry #stack   the simulated operand stack after the instruction, top first, if known
    entry #offset   the instruction's byte offset within the method's code
  entry .Linearized   one instruction of a linearized listing, with its inlining depth and the method it came from
    detail
        The element of the list `Bytecode#linearize` returns. `#depth` is `0` for the
        method's own instructions and one more for each call followed into a callee;
        `#source` names the callee as `owner.method`, and is empty at depth `0`;
        `#instruction` is the instruction itself. A `List[Linearized]` renders, with a
        `BytecodePalette`, as an indented listing.
    entry .teletypeable   renders a linearized listing, indented two spaces per level of depth
        The `Teletypeable` given for `List[Bytecode.Linearized]`, needing a `BytecodePalette`:
        each line is indented by its depth, prefixed with its source method in the palette's
        `bytecode` colour, and followed by the opcode.
    entry #depth   how many calls deep the instruction is: `0` in the listed method itself
    entry #source   the `owner.method` the instruction was inlined from, or empty at depth `0`
    entry #instruction   the instruction
  entry .Opcode   every JVM instruction as an enum case, with its operands decoded
    detail
        One case per opcode of the JVM instruction set, in opcode order, so a case's
        `ordinal` is its opcode number: `Nop` (0) through `Jsr`, `GotoW` (200) and `JsrW`
        (201), with the reserved `Breakpoint`, `Impdep1`, `Impdep2` and the unassigned
        `OpCb` … `OpFd` filling the gaps. Operands are decoded into fields: a load or store
        carries its `slot`; `Bipush`, `Sipush`, `Ldc`, `LdcW` and `Ldc2W` their `value`;
        every branch its `target` as a byte offset; `Tableswitch` and `Lookupswitch` their
        tables; a field instruction its `owner`, `field` and `descriptor`; an invocation its
        `owner`, `method` and `descriptor` (`Invokeinterface` also its `count`, and
        `Invokedynamic` only `method` and `descriptor`); `New`, `Checkcast`, `Instanceof` and
        `Anewarray` their class; `Newarray` its element `Frame`; `Multianewarray` its class
        and `dimensions`; `Iinc` its `slot` and `const`.

        Class names are binary names with `.`, as `java.lang.String`; an `Invokedynamic`
        method name is demangled with `StackTrace.rewrite`, so a lambda call site reads as
        `λ₁` rather than `$anonfun$1`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val call = Bytecode.Opcode.Invokevirtual(t"java.lang.String", t"length", t"()I")
        call.show         // t"invoke·virtual java.lang.String ⌗ length"
        call.cost         // 2
        call.transform(List(Bytecode.Frame.L(t"java.lang.String")))   // List(Bytecode.Frame.I)
        ```

        An opcode is `Showable` as its mnemonic, written with `·` between the parts of a
        compound name and subscripts for constants and slots (`a·load₀`, `i·const₋₁`,
        `dup²∕₁`), followed by its operands; and `Teletypeable`, which colours the mnemonic by
        `#cost` and italicises the operands. Both givens are anonymous, in the companion, so
        they are always in scope. An `Opcode` is read from the JDK's instruction model with
        `.apply`.
    entry .apply   decodes a `java.lang.classfile.Instruction` to an `Opcode`
        `Opcode(instruction, labels)` maps the JDK's instruction object to the enum case for
        its opcode, decoding its operands. `labels` maps the code's `Label`s to byte offsets,
        so that a branch's `target` is an offset; an instruction whose label is absent from
        the map gets `0`. An opcode the decoder does not recognise is a panic.
    entry #cost   a rough relative cost of the instruction, from `0` to `3`
        A coarse ranking used to colour a listing: `0` for constants, loads, stores, stack
        shuffles and widening conversions; `1` for arithmetic, comparisons, conditional
        branches and static or special calls; `2` for array accesses, field accesses, casts,
        unconditional jumps, returns and virtual calls; `3` for switches, allocations,
        monitors, `athrow`, interface and dynamic calls.
    entry #describe   a one-line description of what the instruction does, for the opcodes it knows
        Describes the constant and push instructions and the reserved opcodes in words —
        `push the int constant 1` — and gives `unrecognized` for the rest.
    entry #highlight   the colour for the instruction's `#cost`, from teal (`0`) to crimson (`3`)
    entry #transform   the operand stack after this instruction, given the stack before it
        The stack simulation: pops what the instruction consumes and pushes what it produces,
        with `Frame.I` for an `int` result, `Frame.L(name)` for a `new` object or a loaded
        field of class type, `Frame.L(t"?")` for a reference the instruction cannot name, and
        a method's return frame for an invocation (parsed from its descriptor). The stack is
        top first. A `Dup` on an empty stack fails; a shuffle on a stack shorter than it
        needs leaves the stack as it was.
  entry .Error   raised by `disassemble` when the compiled class file cannot be opened or read
    detail
        Error 295, _the bytecode could not be extracted because …_, with a `#reason`:
        `ClassfileMissing` (295.1, _the generated classfile could not be opened_) when the
        staging compiler left no class file to read, and `ClassfileUnreadable` (295.2, _the
        generated classfile could not be read_) when the file was truncated or the generated
        method was not found in it. `disassemble` needs a `Tactic[Bytecode.Error]` in scope.
    entry .Reason   why extraction failed: `ClassfileMissing` or `ClassfileUnreadable`
        A `Clarification` whose `number` is the error's sub-number: `ClassfileMissing` is `1`
        and `ClassfileUnreadable` is `2`.
    entry .communicable   renders a `Reason` as the message the error embeds
    entry #reason   the reason
  entry #sourceFile   the name of the source file the method's class was compiled from, if recorded
  entry #instructions   the method's instructions, in code order
  entry #maxStack   the maximum operand stack depth the method declares
  entry #maxLocals   the number of local variable slots the method declares
  entry #embed   shifts the listing's line numbers to a `Codepoint`, as if the code were written there
      `bytecode.embed(codepoint)` adds the codepoint's line (less one) to every instruction's
      `line`, and replaces `sourceFile` with the last segment of the codepoint's source path.
      `disassemble` uses it so that the bytecode of a block compiled in a temporary file is
      reported against the file and line the block was written at.
  entry #linearize   follows calls into their callees' bytecode, to a bounded depth and length
      `bytecode.linearize(resolver, maxDepth, maxInstructions)` walks the instructions and,
      at each `Invokestatic` and at each `Invokevirtual` or `Invokeinterface` which
      `#effectivelyStaticCalls` has shown to dispatch to a known class, asks `resolver(owner,
      method, descriptor)` for the callee's `Bytecode` and lists its instructions in place,
      one level deeper, after the call instruction. The resolver returns `Unset` for a method
      it cannot find, and the call is then listed alone. `maxDepth` (default `3`) bounds the
      nesting and `maxInstructions` (default `1000`) the total length of the listing.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val nop = Bytecode.Instruction(Bytecode.Opcode.Nop, Unset, Nil, 0)
      val caller = Bytecode(Unset, List.fill(50)(nop), 0, 0)

      caller.linearize((_, _, _) => Unset, maxInstructions = 7).size   // 7
      ```

      A resolver over the classpath is written with `Classfile`: find the owner's class file,
      seek the method by name, and take its `bytecode`.
  entry #effectivelyStaticCalls   the offsets of the virtual and interface calls whose receiver is a known singleton
      A call through `invokevirtual` or `invokeinterface` whose receiver, in the simulated
      stack before the call, is a `Frame.L(owner)` of exactly the call's own owner class —
      as a call on a Scala `object`'s `MODULE$` is — can only dispatch to that class, so it
      may be followed like a static call. The result is the set of such calls' byte offsets;
      a receiver of unknown class (`L(t"?")`) or of a different class is excluded, as is a call
      whose simulated stack is too shallow to hold its receiver.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val module = Bytecode.Frame.L(t"Foo$$")
      val load = Bytecode.Instruction
        (Bytecode.Opcode.Getstatic(t"Foo$$", t"MODULE$$", t"LFoo$$;"), Unset, List(module), 0)
      val call = Bytecode.Instruction
        (Bytecode.Opcode.Invokevirtual(t"Foo$$", t"doIt", t"()V"), Unset, Nil, 3)

      Bytecode(Unset, List(load, call), 1, 0).effectivelyStaticCalls   // Set(3)
      ```

entry .ClassSurface   the declaration surface of a class: what a compiled consumer links against, and nothing else
  detail
      A `ClassSurface` is a class file reduced to its **linkage surface**: its internal
      `#name`, access `#flags`, `#superclass`, `#interfaces`, generic `#signature`, and its
      `#members` — fields and methods as `ClassSurface.Member`s, each with name, descriptor,
      flags, generic signature, declared exceptions and inlinable constant. `Code`,
      `SourceFile`, `LineNumberTable` and annotations are deliberately dropped: two builds of
      the same source may differ in all of them without changing what a consumer binds to.
      This is the view for comparing releases for binary compatibility, which is why members
      are sorted by selector and interfaces and exceptions by name, rather than kept in file
      order, which is an artifact of the compilation.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val surface: ClassSurface = ClassSurface(cp"/scala/Option.class".read[Data])

      surface.name                       // t"scala/Option"
      surface.supertypes                 // superclass first, then interfaces
      surface.members.filter(_.visible).map(_.selector)
      ```

      Names are JVM internal names, `java/lang/String`, as every reference inside a class
      file spells them. Accessibility of a nested class is read from the `InnerClasses`
      attribute (`#innerFlags`) where the class's own flags would misreport a `protected`
      member class as package-private.
  refer mandible.ClasspathIndex
  refer mandible.Classfile
  entry .Kind   whether a member is a `Method` or a `Field`
  entry .Member   one field or method of a class, as the JVM resolves it
    detail
        A member has a `#kind`, its `#name`, its `#descriptor` (such as `(I)V` or `Ljava/lang/String;`),
        its access `#flags`, an optional generic `#signature`, the sorted internal names of
        its declared `#exceptions` (methods only) and, for a `static final` field, the
        `#constant` its `ConstantValue` attribute carries. Predicates read the flag bits;
        `#visible` is whether the member is `public` or `protected`, the only accessibilities
        a consumer outside the package can link to.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val member = ClassSurface.Member(ClassSurface.Kind.Method, t"length", t"()I", 1)
        member.selector   // t"#length:()I"
        member.public     // true
        ```
    entry #kind   `Kind.Method` or `Kind.Field`
    entry #name   the member's name, as compiled
    entry #descriptor   the JVM descriptor: a method's `(params)result` or a field's type
    entry #flags   the `access_flags` bitmask
    entry #signature   the generic `Signature` attribute, kept because erasure loses what a recompiling consumer needs
    entry #exceptions   the internal names of the `Exceptions` attribute, sorted; `Nil` for a field
    entry #constant   a `static final` field's `ConstantValue`, as `class:value`, for example `java.lang.Integer:1`
        The constant's class is folded in with its value, since `1` as an `Integer` and `"1"`
        as a `String` are different contracts whose `toString`s coincide.
    entry #selector   the member's key within its class: the name and descriptor, prefixed `#` for a method or `.` for a field
        The sigil keeps a method and a field of the same name apart, and the descriptor keeps
        overloads apart, so a selector identifies one member of a class.
    entry #public   whether `ACC_PUBLIC` is set
    entry #protect   whether `ACC_PROTECTED` is set
    entry #priv   whether `ACC_PRIVATE` is set
    entry #static   whether `ACC_STATIC` is set
    entry #finl   whether `ACC_FINAL` is set
    entry #abstrakt   whether `ACC_ABSTRACT` is set
    entry #bridge   whether `ACC_BRIDGE` is set: a compiler-generated bridge method
    entry #synthetic   whether `ACC_SYNTHETIC` is set
    entry #visible   whether the member is `public` or `protected`: linkable from outside its package
    entry #inlinable   whether a consumer's compiler may already have copied this field's value into its own constant pool
        True for a `static final` field of primitive or `String` type which has a
        `#constant`: by the JLS's rules a compiler inlines such a constant at every use, so
        changing its value does not break linkage, but does change consumers' behaviour
        until they are recompiled. A `ConstantValue` on any other field is not inlined, so
        the attribute alone is not the test.
  entry .apply   parses the bytes of a class file to its `ClassSurface`
      `ClassSurface(data)` reads the class file through `java.lang.classfile` and keeps only
      the linkage surface. Fields are read with their signature and constant, methods with
      their signature and exceptions; members are sorted by `Member#selector` and interfaces
      by name. The bytes are not retained.
  entry #name   the class's internal name, `scala/Option`
  entry #flags   the class's `access_flags` bitmask, as the class file records it
  entry #superclass   the superclass's internal name, or `Unset` for `java/lang/Object` itself
  entry #interfaces   the internal names of the directly implemented interfaces, sorted
  entry #signature   the class's generic `Signature` attribute, if any
  entry #members   the fields and methods, sorted by `Member#selector`
  entry #innerFlags   the flags the `InnerClasses` attribute records for this class, if it is nested
      A nested class's own `access_flags` show the outer view, where a `protected` member
      class is neither public nor protected; its real accessibility is in the enclosing
      class's `InnerClasses` entry for it, which this reads back. `Unset` for a top-level
      class.
  entry #interface   whether the class is an interface (`ACC_INTERFACE`)
  entry #isFinal   whether the class is final (`ACC_FINAL`)
  entry #synthetic   whether the class is compiler-generated (`ACC_SYNTHETIC`)
  entry #access   the flags which decide accessibility: `#innerFlags` where present, else `#flags`
  entry #visible   whether the class is `public` or `protected` by its `#access` flags
  entry #supertypes   the directly named supertypes, superclass first, then interfaces
      The order the JVM's own resolution walks, and the order inherited members are keyed
      in by `ClasspathIndex#closure`.
  entry #member   the member with a given `Member#selector`, or `Unset`

entry .ClasspathIndex   resolves internal class names to their surfaces, over in-memory classes plus a classpath
  detail
      A `ClasspathIndex` answers "what does class `name` declare?" for every class a set of
      classes may refer to. It holds the surfaces of the classes it is given eagerly — a
      release's own, which are the ones being examined — and resolves everything else lazily
      by reading `name.class` from a `URLClassLoader` over the classpath entries, with the
      platform classloader as its parent so the JDK's own classes resolve too. Every
      resolution, including a failed one, is memoized, which keeps a walk over many classes'
      supertypes linear.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val own = Map(t"com/example/Main" -> ClassSurface(cp"/com/example/Main.class".read[Data]))
      val index = ClasspathIndex(own, List(t"/lib/dependency.jar"))

      index(t"java/lang/Object")
      index.closure(t"com/example/Main")
      ```

      A class in the in-memory map is never shadowed by a same-named class earlier on the
      classpath.
  refer mandible.ClassSurface
  entry .apply   an index over a map of surfaces by internal name, plus classpath entries as text
      Each classpath entry is a directory or JAR path, as text; a directory must exist on
      disk for the loader to treat it as one. An entry which does not exist resolves nothing.
  entry #own   the internal names of the classes held in memory, sorted
  entry #apply   the surface of a class by internal name, or `Unset` if nothing can supply it
      Looks in the in-memory map first, then reads the class file from the classpath,
      caching the answer either way.
  entry #closure   a class's transitive supertypes, nearest first, and the names which could not be resolved
      `index.closure(name)` walks `ClassSurface#supertypes` breadth-first from `name`,
      excluding `name` itself, and returns a pair: the supertypes found, in order of
      discovery, and the names of those which no source could resolve. An unresolvable
      supertype is reported rather than skipped, because without it the class's inherited
      member set is unknown.

entry .disassemble   compiles a quoted block with the staging compiler and reads its bytecode back
  detail
      `disassemble(code)` takes a `Quotes ?=> Expr[Any]` — a block of code quoted with `'{…}` —
      wraps it in a method, compiles it with the staging compiler into a temporary directory,
      reads the resulting class file and returns the method's `Bytecode`, with its line
      numbers shifted by `Bytecode#embed` to the call site's `Codepoint`, so the listing reads
      against the file the block was written in.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

      val bytecode: Bytecode = disassemble('{ (1 to 10).map(_ * 2).sum })
      bytecode.instructions.map(_.opcode.show)
      ```

      It needs a `TemporaryDirectory` for the compiler's output, a `Classloader` for the
      compiler to load against (its `java` instance is handed to the staging compiler), and a
      `Tactic[Bytecode.Error]`, raised as `ClassfileMissing` when no class file appears and
      `ClassfileUnreadable` when it cannot be read or the generated method is not in it. The
      block is compiled with the experimental language flag enabled, under Scala 3.6's
      settings. Compilation takes a moment, so this is a tool for examining what the compiler
      and its optimizer produce, not for a hot path.
  refer mandible.Bytecode
  refer mandible.Classfile

entry .BytecodePalette   the colours a bytecode listing is rendered with
    A `Palette` in sRGB with, beyond the `background` and `foreground` every palette has, three colours: `bytecode`, for the source-file reference and the
    method a linearized instruction came from; `sourceCode`, for line numbers; and `outline`,
    for the frame of the stack column. A `BytecodePalette` must be in scope to render a
    `Bytecode`, a `List[Bytecode.Frame]` or a `List[Bytecode.Linearized]` as `Teletype`; the
    library supplies no default, so a program defines one. Opcode colours are not in the
    palette: they come from `Bytecode.Opcode#highlight`, by cost.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    given palette: BytecodePalette = new Palette:
      type Form = Srgb
      def background: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Black
      def foreground: Color in Srgb = WebColors.White
      def bytecode: Color in Srgb   = WebColors.Teal
      def sourceCode: Color in Srgb = WebColors.YellowGreen
      def outline: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.Gray
    ```

entry .internal   the home of a macro which disassembles a method selected by a lambda
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Holds `disassemble`, a macro implementation which, given a lambda
      of the form `_.method` over a type and a `Classloader` expression, splices a lookup of
      that method's `Bytecode` through `Classfile`; no public inline entry point currently
      invokes it.
  entry .disassemble   the macro: a `target => Any` selecting a method, to that method's bytecode
      Halts with _this type of lambda is not supported_ (code 91) for a lambda which is not a
      plain member selection.
